ons goal, not a clean-energy mandate, and he never says to end both clean-energy subsidies and mandates. Rung 5 needs both.\n- **The page that would seat rung 5 can't be used.** His campaign page says \"Steve Hilton will repeal California's costly wind and solar mandates and subsidies\". That matches both clauses of rung 5, but it is written in the third person. Under V1, third-person campaign copy counts as someone else describing him, so it can't support a chair. The PG&E plan page and the carbon-sequestration page are third person too.\n- **Other passages:**\n  - Both copies of the CBS LA gas-price interview are about the low-carbon fuel standard, cap-and-trade and refinery rules. That's transport fuel and carbon pricing, so I coded them `adjacent`.\n  - The CBS/SF Examiner debate and the Commonwealth Club interview don't name a clean-energy policy, so I coded them `rhetorical`.\n  - The OnTheIssues pointer, the CalMatters excerpt and the near-empty own-site page are `not-evidence`.\n- **needs_source:** Hilton's own first-person words calling to end both California's clean-energy mandates and its subsidies. A first-person version of the \"Ending Wind and Solar Farm Mandates and Subsidies\" plan would do.\n- **Quotes:**\n  - The net-zero line is `lever` / `lever-named`.\n  - The natural-gas line (\"We will use California natural gas to generate electricity…\") is `direction`.\n  - \"common sense on climate change not ideology\" is `none` and not quotable, flagged `non-differentiating-goal`.\n\nThis blank turns entirely on the third-person rule. If a reviewer counts the campaign's own policy page as Hilton's words, the row would likely become rung 5.","ttft_ms":20856,"type":"result","duration_ms":57227,"uuid":"3c6cfcaf-3f70-4a6e-b14d-c192f7ee88c3","ttft_stream_ms":1367,"time_to_request_ms":108,"first_content_frame_ms":1367,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
